--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/CAPPED_WALKING_CLOSURE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,97 @@
+# Capped-only walking closure candidate
+
+## Scope and unchanged paths
+
+Unrestricted `BuildFlash`, ordinary TP/scalable TP, `BuildTREX`, and
+`BuildTREXStaticRulesCandidate` retain the complete walking metric and existing
+nonbinding-cap checks. No resource limit is increased.
+
+The fixed-destination TP constructors (fresh, retained and explicit source-domain)
+know all four role caps. Their private FLASH backend now retains exact shortest
+paths up to `max(Access, Transfer, Egress, Direct)` and prepares TB transfers only
+up to `Transfer`. They already make the destination absorbing before either
+construction. Their one-cell static-rule restriction remains unchanged.
+
+`BuildBoundedWalkingTB` and `BuildTREXFixedTransferCapRankedCandidate` prepare only
+exact walks up to the fixed transfer cap. Access/direct and egress use immutable
+query-local exact forward searches, independently bounded by their own roles.
+The former builder makes its fixed destination absorbing first. The latter's
+transfer graph is still destination-independent and still requires the existing
+per-query witness replay and complete independent frontier certification. This
+change is not a theorem of ranked cap preservation and does not remove that gate.
+
+## Why radius truncation is exact
+
+Footpath durations are strictly positive. If a directed path has total duration
+at most C, every prefix also has duration at most C. Dijkstra may therefore omit
+an over-C relaxation without losing a feasible endpoint or any needed
+intermediate vertex. The bound is on the total contiguous walking block, not on
+each edge, and walking-only vertices are still expanded. Zero-duration self
+paths are retained even when C is zero.
+
+Every walking block of a supported fixed-role TP query is either access,
+transfer, egress or direct. Each is within its role cap and hence within their
+maximum. Removing paths longer than that maximum cannot remove an eligible
+block; preparing transfer links at Transfer cannot remove an eligible transfer.
+The TP graph's alternating ride/walk state does not permit repeated walking
+arcs to reset a role cap. Static stop-pair rules apply after the whole block as
+before. No transitive-metric transfer reduction is introduced.
+
+For TREX's capped adapters, only transfers consume the prepared bounded metric.
+Forward access search stops expansion at the query destination and separates
+Direct from Access at the endpoint. If Egress exceeds the transfer radius, its forward search ends at the
+destination and is cached per alighting source for the query. Otherwise the
+prepared endpoint lookup already contains its exact shortest path; positive
+paths ending at destination cannot visit it earlier and then leave again. Both traverse original directed
+edges and preserve predecessor selection by distance, stop-ID heap tie and
+footpath-ID adjacency order. TREX partitions still use weak components of the
+raw graph, never of the truncated closure.
+
+## Legacy queries and accounting
+
+`walking_closure_limit` explicitly identifies bounded storage. In its presence,
+`closure_diameter` is the largest retained shortest distance, not the complete
+metric diameter. Snapshot and compact cost diagnostics expose the limit.
+
+The ordinary nonbinding query entrypoints exposed by a fixed-cap TREX index must
+not certify against that truncated diameter. They reconstruct the complete
+metric in a query-local shallow view, retaining the prepared transfer graph,
+partition, ranks and fixed-cap identity. Previously supported full-metric checks
+and representatives are preserved, subject to visible resource exhaustion.
+The fallback cannot mutate the prepared index or recurse. Its construction work
+is charged against MaxQueryWork, and its original closure/path/settlement guards
+remain enforced. Capped endpoint search is likewise charged against query work
+and settlement limits, with `endpoint_walk_work` and `endpoint_walk_settled`
+reported separately. Exact old logical-budget equivalence on fixed-cap indexes
+is not claimed. Unrestricted builders and their counters are unchanged.
+
+## Validation design
+
+The new tests independently enumerate simple raw directed paths to compare the
+entire bounded distance relation. Integration tests enumerate physically
+contiguous raw-event words and assess complete itinerary semantics using the
+pre-existing independent specification, not another router. Cases cover:
+
+- Access, Egress and Direct larger than Transfer.
+- Multi-edge walks, exact-bound equality, zero-radius self paths and directed
+  asymmetry through walking-only intermediate nodes.
+- A transfer whose individual edges fit but whose total block exceeds its cap.
+- A shortest path through destination with a longer valid avoiding detour.
+- Fresh/retained fixed-role TP, bounded TB and the mandatory verified fixed-cap
+  TREX gate.
+- Full-metric legacy entrypoints, explicit work/settlement/cancellation failure,
+  owned radius metadata and concurrent immutable queries.
+
+Endpoint-specific checks additionally cover 16 generated seven-vertex directed
+graphs, four radii, parallel/equal-cost alternatives, destination absorption and
+target-only searches. Distances come from independent simple-path enumeration;
+representatives are compared with the original complete closure. Both lazy
+endpoint queries and complete-metric legacy fallback are exercised concurrently.
+
+Fresh recovery validation on 2026-10-21 with Go 1.27.3, GOMAXPROCS=4 and CPU 0-3
+affinity passed: `go test -p=4 -count=1 -timeout=360s -run TestWalkingScope -v
+./tripbased`, `go test -p=4 -count=1 -timeout=360s ./...`, `go test -race -p=4
+-count=1 -timeout=360s ./...`, `go vet -p=4 ./...`, and `git diff --check`.
+The interrupted older logs are not treated as completed gates. Public SG
+measurements belong to the separately serialized benchmark campaign; these
+correctness results are not performance claims.
